course: dictionary
lesson: 7207
title: Words for places and people
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pharmacy is a place where medicines are sold. Often it is part of a shop, and you can take a doctor's prescription there to get your medicine. When you meet the word pharmacy in a text, look at the words near it for clues. «For small problems, your grown-up can often go straight to the pharmacy.» Small problems and going straight there tell you a pharmacy is a place you can go to for help with health.
explain: The same place can have different names. In the UK, people say «chemist's» for the same shop. In the US, they say «drugstore». If you read a story from another country and see an unknown name for a shop, ask yourself: what would a person buy or get there? The clues will help you.
explain: Pharmacy can also mean a study or a job. Someone who works with medicines, and makes them up for people, is a pharmacist. Look at how the word is used in the sentence to know which meaning the writer wants.
explain: Some words are full of safety. «Old or unwanted medicines should go back to the pharmacy, not in the bin or down the toilet.» The word should tells you what to do, and the reason, where they can harm people, animals or rivers, tells you why. Always follow the advice, and only take medicine with an adult's help.
fact: A pharmacy is a shop, or part of a shop, where medicines are sold and prescriptions are given out.
fact: «Pharmacy» can also mean the study and job of preparing medicines.
fact: In the UK, a pharmacy is also called a «chemist's»; in the US, it is called a «drugstore».
fact: Old or unwanted medicines should go back to the pharmacy, not in the bin or down the toilet, because they can harm people, animals and rivers.
fact: In the UK, medicines are sorted into three groups: those sold in any shop, those sold only in a pharmacy, and those given only with a prescription.
exercise: «Old or unwanted medicines should go back to the pharmacy, not in the bin.» Where should old medicines go? | In the river | In the bin | *Back to the pharmacy | Down the toilet
exercise: What is a pharmacy? | A school for doctors | *A shop, or part of a shop, where medicines are sold | A kind of cloud | A park with a playground
exercise: In the UK, what is another name for a pharmacy? | Drugstore | *Chemist's | Post office | Bakery
test: «For a mild rash, your grown-up can often go straight to the pharmacy.» What does mild suggest about the rash? | Hot and sticky | *Not very serious | Huge and bright red | Very dangerous
test: Besides a shop, what else can pharmacy mean? | A sports team | *The study and job of preparing medicines | A dance | A type of weather
exam: Why should you follow the label on a medicine? | Because it makes the medicine stronger | Because the label is the medicine itself | *Because it tells you how to take the medicine safely | Because labels are pretty to look at
